WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.600
G7 nations will run joint drills against AI-driven cyber attacks that could hit power grids, banks, and hospitals across member countries. This is Omni View, Episode 207. Group of seven governments have begun assigning national agencies to prepare testing ranges and share technical protocols for those exercises. Reuters described the plan as a practical step to share technical knowledge among the seven largest advanced economies. Officials in Tokyo and Ottawa have already signaled they will contribute

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.240
national testing ranges that simulate attacks on payment systems and energy grids.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:39.700
The initiative sits alongside existing bilateral agreements between the United States and Japan

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:45.480
on semiconductor supply security. The shared ground is that the seven are preparing to practice

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:51.640
together, using national ranges and common technical protocols. European participants want

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:56.800
those exercises to reduce reliance on single vendor cloud services that have proved vulnerable

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:02.660
in past incidents. Asian members want the same drills kept on defensive capabilities rather than

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:08.240
offensive ones. For the European governments involved, a joint test is a way to stop depending on

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:14.320
one cloud vendor after earlier failures showed how that concentration can go wrong. For Asian members,

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:19.900
a joint test is only useful if it stays on how to withstand an attack, not how to mount one.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:25.280
The work builds on lessons from earlier ransomware events that prompted several countries to update

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.460
their domestic incident response rules.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:33.430
Al Jazeera has been examining how artificial intelligence coding tools are raising new security

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:35.510
questions for the same kinds of systems.

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:41.330
Each member keeps full control over its own networks, so shared evaluation criteria would

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:43.990
be adopted voluntarily, not through a treaty.

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:49.670
United States Secretary of State Marco Rubio spoke by phone with Lebanese President Joseph

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:55.970
of Ione, and press for assurances that 780 kg of explosives intercepted last week will

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:57.450
not be returned to Hezbollah.

00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:03.090
Al-Jazeera reported that Hezbollah lawmaker Hassan Fadlala confirmed the group's link to

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:07.270
the cargo, but demanded its return and the release of detained personnel.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:11.310
The shipment was stopped on a highway between Beirut and the Bekha Valley.

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:18.230
The load included 250 kg of TNT and 550 lbs of C4 plastic explosives.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:23.290
I own said security forces are continuing to seize illicit weapons across the country,

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:27.630
and Lebanese judicial officials have opened an investigation into the transport.

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:32.490
United States officials describe the call as part of efforts to prevent rearmament

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:33.730
of non-state actors.

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:38.950
From Washington's vantage, keeping that cargo out of Hezbollah's hands is how you stop

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:41.870
a non-state group from building up again after a seizure.

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:47.090
Fatal Law's demand is that the cargo and the detained personnel go back to the group

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:49.630
that in his account was tied to the shipment.

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:55.310
The Lebanese presidency stated that Ione asked Rubio to press Israel to stop strikes in

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:56.030
southern Lebanon.

00:02:56.650 --> 00:03:01.850
Prime Minister Nawaf Salam described the transport as a criminal operation and praised security

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:03.630
forces for intercepting the vehicle.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:09.290
AP News placed the seizure in a wider regional security setting, while Lebanese authorities

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:12.890
continue the judicial investigation into how the explosives moved.

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:18.434
leaders in the Association of Southeast Asian Nations, or ASEAN, have begun

00:03:18.434 --> 00:03:23.154
engaging again with Mayan-Mars' coup leader Min Ang-Lang, despite ongoing

00:03:23.154 --> 00:03:27.534
fighting inside the country. Al-Jazeera noted that some neighbors see practical

00:03:27.534 --> 00:03:31.714
value in keeping communication channels open to manage border flows and trade.

00:03:32.394 --> 00:03:36.294
Other governments in the block have maintained a stricter distance, citing the

00:03:36.294 --> 00:03:40.514
military's record of targeting civilians. Fighting between the military and

00:03:40.514 --> 00:03:42.914
armed opposition groups shows no sign of ending,

00:03:43.374 --> 00:03:45.994
and the military continues bombing civilian areas.

00:03:46.614 --> 00:03:48.474
Critics inside and outside the region

00:03:48.474 --> 00:03:51.414
say the contacts risk normalizing military rule,

00:03:51.834 --> 00:03:53.354
while those airstrikes go on,

00:03:53.814 --> 00:03:55.354
and they warn that renewed acceptance

00:03:55.354 --> 00:03:56.714
could embolden the leadership.

00:03:57.274 --> 00:03:59.654
Everything turns on whether talking to the junta

00:03:59.654 --> 00:04:01.994
is a tool for handling refugees and commerce

00:04:02.444 --> 00:04:04.314
or a grant of political acceptance

00:04:04.314 --> 00:04:06.734
while civilian areas are still being bombed.

00:04:07.334 --> 00:04:09.494
Neighboring states have cited refugee movements

00:04:09.494 --> 00:04:12.874
and cross-border commerce as reasons for the renewed contact.

00:04:13.494 --> 00:04:18.194
ASEAN's longstanding non-interference principle still limits how much collective pressure the

00:04:18.194 --> 00:04:21.594
block can apply, even for governments that want more distance.

00:04:22.314 --> 00:04:25.114
The fighting has not paused while those conversations resume.

00:04:26.278 --> 00:04:30.158
Alberta's provincial government will hold a vote on whether to begin the legal process

00:04:30.158 --> 00:04:31.558
of separating from Canada.

00:04:32.338 --> 00:04:36.698
Deutscheweller reported Prime Minister Mark Karnie's remarks as a direct response to that

00:04:36.698 --> 00:04:39.678
announcement, including his claim that the United States

00:04:39.678 --> 00:04:45.338
appears to be encouraging the move. The oil-rich province has long debated greater autonomy over

00:04:45.338 --> 00:04:51.138
energy policy and federal carbon pricing rules, and its energy sector has repeatedly clashed with

00:04:51.138 --> 00:04:56.638
national climate targets in recent years. The strongest case for the vote from its supporters is

00:04:56.638 --> 00:05:01.278
that federal rules on carbon pricing and pipeline approvals harm provincial revenues.

00:05:01.978 --> 00:05:07.238
Opponents say separation would disrupt integrated energy markets and raise costs for both Alberta

00:05:07.238 --> 00:05:08.518
and the rest of Canada.

00:05:09.278 --> 00:05:13.718
Supporters treat the ballot as a legal path to regain control over energy income.

00:05:13.898 --> 00:05:15.998
They say Ottawa's climate rules are draining.

00:05:16.778 --> 00:05:21.238
Opponents treat the same ballot as a break in a shared energy market that would leave households

00:05:21.238 --> 00:05:24.538
and industry on both sides of the provincial line paying more.

00:05:25.298 --> 00:05:28.338
The referendum question itself remains under legal review.

00:05:29.205 --> 00:05:33.825
Bank of England Governor Andrew Bailey told lawmakers it is too early to judge whether

00:05:33.825 --> 00:05:38.965
higher energy prices from Middle East conflict will feed into broader wage and price increases.

00:05:39.645 --> 00:05:44.645
The Wall Street Journal reported that testimony as cautious, noting that energy costs have

00:05:44.645 --> 00:05:50.065
risen, but core services inflation has not yet accelerated. The United Kingdom has already

00:05:50.065 --> 00:05:55.385
recorded the first round of energy-driven inflation from that conflict. Some members of the Banks

00:05:55.385 --> 00:06:00.385
Monetary Policy Committee, the group that sets interest rates, have argued for waiting

00:06:00.385 --> 00:06:03.285
until clearer data arrive before adjusting rates.

00:06:03.845 --> 00:06:07.665
Others on the committee have highlighted the risk that persistent energy shocks could

00:06:07.665 --> 00:06:09.585
embed higher inflation expectations.

00:06:10.325 --> 00:06:14.625
Committee members continue to weigh whether those second-round effects will show up in wage

00:06:14.625 --> 00:06:16.825
negotiations or in consumer prices.

00:06:17.853 --> 00:06:23.073
Jupiter asset management fund manager Harry Richards said he is not buying United States government

00:06:23.073 --> 00:06:28.373
bonds, even as yields reach multi-decade highs that have attracted other large investors.

00:06:28.373 --> 00:06:33.493
Bloomberg reported that many pension and insurance funds have increased Treasury holdings

00:06:33.493 --> 00:06:35.413
for the higher income they now provide.

00:06:35.953 --> 00:06:41.233
Richard cited concerns over fiscal deficits and potential volatility, and Treasury prices

00:06:41.233 --> 00:06:44.893
have fluctuated this month as investors weigh federal reserve signals.

00:06:45.473 --> 00:06:49.913
He has kept a preference for shorter duration credit and selected emerging market debt.

00:06:50.453 --> 00:06:54.933
Some of the world's biggest funds are warming to United States Treasuries as yields climb,

00:06:55.473 --> 00:06:57.193
seeing a chance to lock in returns.

00:06:57.193 --> 00:06:57.453
Returns.

00:06:57.893 --> 00:07:02.653
Richard Stance highlights caution among managers who think today's yields still may not cover

00:07:02.653 --> 00:07:04.593
long-term inflation and debt risks.

00:07:05.775 --> 00:07:10.955
Mie prefecture enacted Japan's first local penalties for customer harassment, progress

00:07:10.955 --> 00:07:15.395
worth knowing for retail and service workers who have faced a rise in abusive behavior.

00:07:15.995 --> 00:07:20.635
The Japan Times reported that the ordinance defines harassment as repeated unreasonable

00:07:20.635 --> 00:07:25.135
demands or abusive language and sets fines of up to 50,000 yen.

00:07:25.135 --> 00:07:25.235
Japan.

00:07:25.795 --> 00:07:30.055
Local officials say the measure responds to rising complaints from frontline staff in

00:07:30.055 --> 00:07:31.415
retail and service jobs.

00:07:32.055 --> 00:07:36.355
Business groups welcomed clearer guidelines while noting that enforcement will depend on

00:07:36.355 --> 00:07:37.555
victims coming forward.

00:07:38.255 --> 00:07:40.915
Labor unions have called for similar rules nationwide.

00:07:41.495 --> 00:07:46.235
The prefectural government stated that this is the first ordinance in Japan to impose penalties

00:07:46.235 --> 00:07:47.475
for customer harassment.

00:07:48.115 --> 00:07:53.595
The prefecture will begin applying the penalties in April 2027, after staff training.

00:07:53.595 --> 00:07:59.818
To really understand how a joint cyber exercise becomes everyday national practice, you have

00:07:59.818 --> 00:08:03.678
to follow the technical working groups that meet for months after the cameras leave.

00:08:04.378 --> 00:08:08.838
A common misunderstanding is to treat a communicate from the 7 as the action itself.

00:08:09.358 --> 00:08:14.058
When the operational work starts only after finance and interior ministries assign a lead

00:08:14.058 --> 00:08:19.258
agency in each country, that lead is often a mix of the National Cybersecurity Center

00:08:19.258 --> 00:08:21.898
and the Central Banks Operational Resilience Team.

00:08:21.898 --> 00:08:28.418
Those agencies then exchange red team scenarios, practice attacks run by a friendly team, and

00:08:28.418 --> 00:08:33.598
they agree on common evaluation criteria so a simulated incident in one country can be scored

00:08:33.598 --> 00:08:35.718
against the same benchmarks used in another.

00:08:36.318 --> 00:08:41.538
They also have to agree on metrics for success, such as time to detect an intrusion or time

00:08:41.538 --> 00:08:43.938
to restore service after a simulated failure.

00:08:44.678 --> 00:08:49.078
Countries differ in their legal frameworks for sharing threat intelligence, so the working

00:08:49.078 --> 00:08:53.218
groups must reconcile data protection rules before any joint drill can proceed.

00:08:53.918 --> 00:08:59.918
The last comparable exercise, held after the 2021 ransomware wave, produced a shared playbook

00:08:59.918 --> 00:09:03.558
that later showed up in how several countries wrote their own response rules.

00:09:04.018 --> 00:09:08.618
Historical patterns show that statements from the seven on financial stability or cyber

00:09:08.618 --> 00:09:13.598
resilience often lead to bilateral follow-ups, rather than immediate collective action.

00:09:14.118 --> 00:09:18.698
That layered sequence is why an announcement can look modest on the day and still change

00:09:18.698 --> 00:09:22.718
national practice later, if agencies choose to adopt what they learned.

00:09:23.298 --> 00:09:27.838
When you hear that the seven will act together, the useful check is whether follow-up working

00:09:27.838 --> 00:09:33.098
groups publish after-action reports, and whether national regulators then cite those reports

00:09:33.098 --> 00:09:33.938
in new rules.

00:09:34.498 --> 00:09:38.678
Look for whether an exercise summary appears on the public websites of at least three

00:09:38.678 --> 00:09:42.158
participating national cyber centers within six months of the drills.

00:09:43.377 --> 00:09:47.577
Reuters treats the exercises as a practical knowledge sharing step among the largest

00:09:47.577 --> 00:09:53.077
advanced economies, while Al Jazeera sets them against the security questions raised by artificial

00:09:53.077 --> 00:09:59.337
intelligence coding tools. The Bank of England's next inflation report, due in November, will be the

00:09:59.337 --> 00:10:04.937
first full look at whether those energy costs are spreading into wages and prices. That's omnivue.

